# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# pluto-not-a-planet (Miya's Note). Script from miya 2026-11-09 (dated 2026-10-29); fact-checked per guideline 12-1 (see script-check.md).
SCENES = [
 ('S01', 'My Very Educated Mother', 'ill', 'p01a', 'Take a moment and think back to your old elementary school classroom. If you were sitting at a wooden desk anytime before 2006, you probably memorized a simple sentence. My Very Educated Mother Just Served Us Nine Pizzas. That last word, pizza, stood for Pluto.'),
 ('S02', 'My Very Educated Mother', 'ill', 'p01b', 'Posters of nine pristine spheres hung on classroom walls across the entire globe. Millions of us grew up believing that the boundary of our solar system was safely guarded by a lonely, icy ninth planet.'),
 ('S03', 'Overnight Change', 'ill', 'p02a', 'Then, almost overnight, the story changed. In the late summer of 2006, breaking news alerts flashed across television screens worldwide. Scientists had gathered in Europe and voted to strip Pluto of its crown. Children wrote angry letters to astronomers, and adults felt a strange sense of personal grief.'),
 ('S04', 'Overnight Change', 'ill', 'p02b', 'How could a beloved planet simply disappear from the official list? Did Pluto physically change, or did we?'),
 ('S05', 'Planet X', 'ill', 'p03a', 'To understand what really happened, we have to travel back nearly a century. In the early decades of the twentieth century, astronomers noticed subtle wobbles in the orbit of Uranus. Even after accounting for the gravitational tug of Neptune, something seemed slightly off. Mathematics suggested another massive world was lurking in the dark freezing depths of space.'),
 ('S06', 'Planet X', 'ill', 'p03b', 'Scientists called this phantom world Planet X. Wealthy businessman and astronomer Percival Lowell poured his fortune into finding it, building an observatory in Flagstaff, Arizona.'),
 ('S07', 'Planet X', 'ill', 'p04a', 'Lowell searched the skies until his death, but the phantom world remained elusive. In 1929, the observatory hired a young farm boy from Kansas named Clyde Tombaugh. Tombaugh possessed no university degree in astronomy, but he had an extraordinary work ethic. Night after night, he endured freezing temperatures to photograph small patches of the night sky through a telescope.'),
 ('S08', 'Planet X', 'ill', 'p04b', 'Each patch was photographed multiple times, spaced several days apart.'),
 ('S09', 'The Blink Comparator', 'ill', 'p05a', 'Tombaugh sat in a darkened room using a machine called a blink comparator. This mechanical device flipped back and forth between two glass photographic plates taken on different nights. Distant stars remained perfectly still, while any moving object would appear to jump back and forth.'),
 ('S10', 'The Blink Comparator', 'ill', 'p05b', 'On February 18, 1930, after examining millions of tiny points of light, Tombaugh spotted a speck shifting position across the background stars. Mankind had discovered the elusive ninth planet.'),
 ('S11', 'A Shrinking Planet', 'ill', 'p06a', 'They named this newfound world Pluto, after the Roman god of the underworld. But right from the beginning, an uncomfortable puzzle began to take shape. Early astronomers assumed Pluto was an enormous giant, perhaps even larger than Earth. After all, it was supposed to be the massive Planet X pulling on Uranus and Neptune.'),
 ('S12', 'A Shrinking Planet', 'ill', 'p06b', 'Yet, as optical telescopes improved over the following decades, Pluto refused to look like a giant. It appeared frustratingly faint, showing up as nothing more than a dim pinpoint of light.'),
 ('S13', 'A Shrinking Planet', 'ill', 'p07a', "With every passing decade, scientific estimates of Pluto's mass dropped dramatically. In 1931, scientists calculated that Pluto might have the mass of Earth. By 1948, new observations suggested it was only about as massive as Mars. The mystery deepened in 1978 when astronomer James Christy noticed a strange bulge on Pluto's images."),
 ('S14', 'A Shrinking Planet', 'ill', 'p07b', "That bulge turned out to be a large moon, later named Charon. The discovery of Charon gave scientists an incredible gift: a precise way to calculate Pluto's true mass."),
 ('S15', 'Planet X Was an Illusion', 'ill', 'p08a', "The mathematical results were stunning. Pluto was not a mighty giant perturbing the orbits of giant planets. In fact, it had virtually no effect on Neptune at all. Later space calculations revealed that the supposed wobble in Uranus was merely a minor mathematical error in Neptune's estimated mass."),
 ('S16', 'Planet X Was an Illusion', 'ill', 'p08b', 'Planet X was an illusion. When the math settled, Pluto turned out to be microscopic by planetary standards.'),
 ('S17', 'Smaller Than the Moon', 'ill', 'p09a', "Let us put the numbers into perspective. Pluto has a diameter of roughly 2,376 kilometers. That means Pluto is noticeably smaller than Earth's Moon, which measures about 3,474 kilometers across. In fact, there are seven moons in our solar system that are larger than Pluto, including Ganymede, Titan, and Europa."),
 ('S18', 'Smaller Than the Moon', 'ill', 'p09b', 'If you laid Pluto across the continental United States, it would stretch about halfway from coast to coast. Could a world smaller than our own Moon truly belong in the same league as Jupiter?'),
 ('S19', 'A Tilted Orbit', 'ill', 'p10a', "Size was only the first red flag. The second problem was Pluto's path through space. If you look at our solar system from above, the major planets orbit in nearly circular paths, resting on a flat geometric plane like marbles rolling around a wide dinner plate."),
 ('S20', 'A Tilted Orbit', 'ill', 'p10b', 'Pluto completely ignores this rule. Its orbit is tilted at an astonishing seventeen-degree angle relative to the rest of the solar system.'),
 ('S21', 'Inside Neptune', 'ill', 'p11a', "Furthermore, Pluto's orbit is deeply elliptical, stretching into an extreme oval shape. Most of the time, it circles the Sun far beyond the realm of Neptune, taking 248 Earth years to complete a single lap. Yet because of its strange oval path, Pluto actually cuts inside the orbit of Neptune for about twenty years during each journey."),
 ('S22', 'Inside Neptune', 'ill', 'p11b', 'Between 1979 and 1999, Pluto was actually closer to the Sun than Neptune was. No other recognized planet crossed the orbital path of its neighbor like this.'),
 ('S23', 'The Kuiper Belt', 'ill', 'p12a', 'For decades, astronomers tolerated these quirks. Pluto was treated like an eccentric oddball in an otherwise orderly celestial family. But science rarely stands still. In 1992, astronomers detected a faint, slow-moving object beyond Neptune, cataloged as 1992 QB1. It was an icy body drifting through the absolute cold of deep space.'),
 ('S24', 'The Kuiper Belt', 'ill', 'p12b', 'Within years, astronomers discovered dozens more, then hundreds. Researchers realized that the outer fringe of the solar system was not empty at all.'),
 ('S25', 'The Kuiper Belt', 'ill', 'p13a', 'This vast ring of frozen debris became known as the Kuiper Belt. It is a gigantic cosmic junkyard, packed with leftover remnants from the dawn of our solar system four and a half billion years ago. As more telescopes scanned this frozen frontier, an unsettling realization began to dawn on astronomers.'),
 ('S26', 'The Kuiper Belt', 'ill', 'p13b', 'Pluto was not a lonely guardian sitting at the edge of nowhere. It was simply the brightest member of a colossal swarm of icy wanderers.'),
 ('S27', 'Eris', 'ill', 'p14a', 'Then came the year 2005, and the crisis reached a breaking point. An astronomical team led by Mike Brown discovered a bright, distant object far beyond Neptune. They named it Eris. When astronomers calculated its dimensions, jaws dropped. Eris appeared to be roughly the same size as Pluto, and measurements indicated it was actually about twenty-seven percent more massive.'),
 ('S28', 'Eris', 'ill', 'p14b', 'If Pluto was an official planet, did that mean Eris had to become the tenth planet?'),
 ('S29', 'Prague', 'ill', 'p15a', 'The scientific community faced an urgent dilemma. Powerful new digital observatories were turning up large icy worlds in the outer rim. If Eris was a planet, then what about Sedna, Makemake, and Haumea? What about the many similar worlds that might still be waiting in the deep dark?'),
 ('S30', 'Prague', 'ill', 'p15b', 'Were school children going to memorize fifty planets? A hundred planets? Two hundred planets? For thousands of years, humans had used the word planet, but no one had ever written down a strict scientific definition.'),
 ('S31', 'Prague', 'ill', 'p16a', 'In August 2006, the International Astronomical Union held its general assembly in the historic city of Prague. Hundreds of astronomers gathered in lecture halls, and the atmosphere was electric. Some argued that historical tradition should keep Pluto on the list.'),
 ('S32', 'Prague', 'ill', 'p16b', 'Others insisted that science cannot prioritize emotional nostalgia over physical truth.'),
 ('S33', 'Three Rules', 'ill', 'p17a', 'After days of intense debate, the astronomers drafted a formal definition. To be officially recognized as a true planet in our solar system, a celestial body must satisfy three strict scientific criteria. The first criterion is simple: the object must orbit directly around the Sun.'),
 ('S34', 'Three Rules', 'ill', 'p17b', 'Moons are excluded because they orbit another planetary body. Both Earth and Pluto easily pass this first test.'),
 ('S35', 'Three Rules', 'ill', 'p18a', 'The second criterion concerns physics and gravity. The object must possess sufficient mass so that its own gravity overcomes rigid body forces, pulling it into a state of hydrostatic equilibrium. In plain English, this means the object must be heavy enough to squash itself into a nearly round, spherical shape.'),
 ('S36', 'Three Rules', 'ill', 'p18b', 'Pluto, having plenty of gravity to maintain a smooth sphere, passed the second rule with flying colors.'),
 ('S37', 'Three Rules', 'ill', 'p19a', "Then came the fateful third rule, the requirement that would seal Pluto's fate. The object must have cleared the neighborhood around its orbit. What does that phrase actually mean? When a true planet forms, its gravitational dominance allows it to either consume, capture, or fling away nearly all other debris sharing its orbital zone."),
 ('S38', 'Three Rules', 'ill', 'p19b', 'A true planet cleans its cosmic room, becoming the undisputed gravitational heavyweight of its orbital highway.'),
 ('S39', 'Clearing the Neighborhood', 'ill', 'p20a', 'Consider planet Earth. If you calculate the combined mass of Earth compared to all other objects sharing its orbital space, Earth outweighs them millions of times over. But when scientists examined Pluto, the numbers collapsed. Pluto makes up only a tiny fraction of the total mass in its orbital neighborhood.'),
 ('S40', 'Clearing the Neighborhood', 'ill', 'p20b', 'It shares its cosmic highway with thousands of other Kuiper Belt objects and remains gravitationally locked in an orbital resonance with Neptune.'),
 ('S41', 'Clearing the Neighborhood', 'ill', 'p21a', 'Pluto failed the third test completely. It had not cleared its neighborhood; it was surrounded by icy peers. On August 24, 2006, the astronomers in Prague cast their final votes. The assembly created a brand-new official category called dwarf planets. A dwarf planet orbits the Sun and is round, but it has not cleared its neighborhood.'),
 ('S42', 'Clearing the Neighborhood', 'ill', 'p21b', 'In a single historic vote, Pluto was demoted from the pantheon of major planets. Our solar system was officially trimmed down to eight.'),
 ('S43', 'The Reaction', 'ill', 'p22a', "Public reaction was explosive. Many people felt as if a piece of their childhood had been stolen. Children wrote letters of protest, and plenty of adults were just as upset. In 2007, the legislature of New Mexico, where Clyde Tombaugh lived for many years, even passed a resolution declaring Pluto a planet in New Mexico's skies."),
 ('S44', 'New Horizons', 'ill', 'p23a', "Yet nine years later, in July 2015, humanity finally got a close look at this distant exile. NASA's New Horizons spacecraft screamed past Pluto after traveling nearly five billion kilometers across the solar system. The images it beamed back to Earth left scientists completely breathless."),
 ('S45', 'New Horizons', 'ill', 'p23b', 'For decades, many had imagined Pluto as a cratered, geologically dead snowball frozen in eternal darkness. Instead, the spacecraft revealed one of the most dynamic, active, and mesmerizing landscapes in the entire solar system.'),
 ('S46', 'New Horizons', 'ill', 'p24a', "New Horizons photographed vast plains of smooth nitrogen ice, glowing softly in the faint sunlight. Scientists identified a vast basin about one thousand kilometers across, named Sputnik Planitia, that forms the left half of a giant heart shape. Mountains made of rock-hard water ice towered up to about three kilometers into the thin haze of Pluto's nitrogen atmosphere."),
 ('S47', 'New Horizons', 'ill', 'p24b', 'There were signs of flowing glaciers, possible ice volcanoes, and hints of a vast liquid water ocean sloshing beneath its frozen crust. Pluto was not a dead rock; it was a breathing, evolving world.'),
 ('S48', 'Not a Demotion', 'ill', 'p25a', 'Did losing its planet title diminish any of this wondrous beauty? Of course not. In scientific reality, demoting Pluto was never an insult. It was one of the greatest triumphs of astronomical discovery. As long as we forced Pluto to be the ninth planet, it was an awkward misfit that ruined our understanding of how planets form.'),
 ('S49', 'Not a Demotion', 'ill', 'p25b', 'But once we recognized it as the dazzling king of the Kuiper Belt, everything fell into place. Pluto unlocked a brand-new frontier of thousands of frozen worlds.'),
 ('S50', 'Still Out There', 'ill', 'p26a', 'Tonight, if you step outside away from city lights and look up into the deep night sky, you can often spot the glow of Jupiter or Saturn. They are mighty worlds of gas and storm. But far beyond their comfortable orbits, in the silent, freezing dark, Pluto continues its slow 248-year journey around our Sun.'),
 ('S51', 'Still Out There', 'ill', 'p26b', 'It carries a gigantic frozen heart on its surface, caring nothing for the names and labels invented by human beings on a small blue marble billions of kilometers away.'),
 ('S52', 'Still Out There', 'ill', 'p27a', 'Science does not honor the cosmos by clinging to comfortable old habits. Science honors the universe by listening to what nature actually tells us. Pluto did not lose its identity on that August day in Prague. Instead, it showed us that our solar system is vastly larger, stranger, and far more wondrous than a neat list of nine names printed in an old school textbook.'),
 ('S53', 'Still Out There', 'ill', 'p27b', 'And that realization is far more magical than any title we could ever bestow.'),
]
